? Why is `#![no_std]` + `#[cfg(feature = "std")] extern crate std;` preferred over `#![cfg_attr(not(feature = "std"), no_std)]`?
- It compiles faster
* The prelude is identical in every configuration, so code can't silently depend on std-only prelude items when the feature is on
- It enables alloc automatically
- cfg_attr doesn't work with no_std
> With cfg_attr, enabling std switches to the std prelude, making it easy to write code that breaks without the feature.

? Your no_std crate depends on `foo`, which has `default = ["std"]`. What should you write?
- `foo = "1"`
* `foo = { version = "1", default-features = false }`, plus forwarding `std = ["foo/std"]` in your own features
- `foo = { version = "1", features = ["no_std"] }`
- Nothing: features are disabled for no_std crates automatically
> Otherwise foo's std feature is on for every user of your crate, and Cargo's feature unification spreads it.

? Why must Cargo features be additive?
* Cargo unifies features across the dependency graph: if any crate enables a feature, every user gets it, so enabling a feature must never remove or change API
- Because features are sorted alphabetically
- Features can't be disabled once published
- To keep compile times low
> A "no_std" feature that removes std support would break other crates in the same build.

? What is the strongest check that a crate and all its dependencies are really no_std?
- `cargo test --no-default-features`
- `grep -r std src/`
* `cargo build --no-default-features --target thumbv7em-none-eabihf` (a target that has no std at all)
- `cargo clippy`
> On the host, a dependency can link std without anyone noticing. A bare-metal target simply doesn't have it.

? Which lint makes any `unsafe` block a hard error that can't be re-allowed further down?
- `#![deny(unsafe_code)]`
* `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]`
- `#![warn(unsafe_op_in_unsafe_fn)]`
- `clippy::undocumented_unsafe_blocks`
> `forbid` can't be overridden by inner `allow` attributes, unlike `deny`.

? Which clippy lint flags `v[i]` because it can panic?
- `clippy::unwrap_used`
* `clippy::indexing_slicing`
- `clippy::as_conversions`
- `clippy::panic`
> It's a restriction lint, off by default, aimed at panic-free code.

? A tool explores your Rust function for *all* inputs up to a bound and proves an assertion can't fail. Which one?
- Miri
- proptest
* Kani
- cargo-fuzz
> Kani is a bounded model checker for Rust. Miri detects UB on executed paths; proptest and fuzzing sample inputs.

? In a firmware release profile, why is `debug = true` harmless for flash size?
* Debug info lives in non-loaded ELF sections; the flash image only contains .text/.rodata/.data
- Because the linker compresses it
- It isn't harmless: it doubles flash usage
- Debug info is stripped by the MCU at boot
> Keep it for debugging with probe-rs/GDB; measure flash usage with `cargo size`, not the ELF file size.

? What does a `rust-toolchain.toml` file do?
- Lists crate dependencies
* Pins the toolchain channel/version, targets and components for everyone building the project
- Configures clippy lints
- Defines the linker script
> Reproducible builds start with a pinned compiler; safety projects pin a qualified version.

? Which tool checks dependencies for licenses, banned crates and security advisories?
- cargo-bloat
* cargo-deny
- cargo-binutils
- cargo-expand
> cargo-deny (and cargo-audit/cargo-vet) help manage supply-chain risk, which also reduces review and qualification effort.
